# sqlite -- required by libsoup3, and the package that arms a time bomb in gnupg.
#
# libsoup3 takes dependency('sqlite3') with no option guard, for its cookie jar
# and HSTS store. That is the only reason it is here.
#
# BUT LANDING IT CHANGES ANOTHER PACKAGE, AND THAT IS WORTH READING BEFORE
# TOUCHING THIS RECIPE. gnupg's TOFU trust model needs sqlite3, is ENABLED BY
# DEFAULT, and gnupg's configure DOES NOT FAIL when sqlite3 is missing -- it sets
# use_tofu=no and carries on. So before this package existed, gnupg's
# --disable-tofu flag was doing nothing observable. THE DAY THIS MERGES, THAT
# FLAG STARTS DOING REAL WORK.
#
# pkgs/gnupg carries that flag and an install-time assertion that the built gpg
# does not link sqlite3, precisely so the transition is checked rather than
# assumed. If that assertion ever fires, this package is why.
#
# The upstream tarball is the "autoconf" amalgamation, which is the packaging
# form rather than the development tree.
#
# THE VERSION SPELLING IS THE REASON THIS RECIPE ARRIVED LAST. sqlite encodes
# 3.50.4 as 3500400 -- major, then minor and patch zero-padded to two digits,
# then a two-digit build. tape needs three-component semver, and check-recipes
# requires the declared version to appear in the source URL, so the two rules
# jointly excluded sqlite until version_forms learned this encoding. Declaring
# 3500400 instead would have satisfied the source check and failed semver, and
# tape would have skipped the package silently.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
SRC_URL=https://www.sqlite.org/2025/sqlite-autoconf-3500400.tar.gz
SRC_SHA256=a3db587a1b92ee5ddac2f66b3edb41b26f9c867275782d46c3a088977d6a5b18
SRC_DIR=sqlite-autoconf-3500400

# SQLITE'S FEATURE SET IS COMPILED IN, NOT CONFIGURED, which makes this recipe
# unlike every other one here: the switches below are -D defines carried in
# CFLAGS rather than configure options, and omitting one does not disable a
# feature so much as leave the library at a much older default.
#
# The four enabled are the ones consumers assume exist:
#   FTS5              full-text search, which GLib-family consumers expect
#   JSON1             JSON functions; on by default in modern sqlite but named
#                     so a version bump cannot quietly remove it
#   COLUMN_METADATA   required by anything calling sqlite3_column_table_name()
#   SECURE_DELETE     overwrite deleted content rather than leaving it in the
#                     page. A cookie jar and an HSTS store are exactly the files
#                     where that matters.
#
# --disable-static IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, not a restatement. sqlite's configure
# reports [default=yes] for static, like libseccomp and unlike the whole GnuPG
# chain where it reports [default=no]. That is the second package in this
# workstream where the same flag flips meaning, and the reason both say so.
#
# --disable-readline: the sqlite shell links readline for line editing and
# readline is not packaged. At its default configure would look for it and
# silently build a shell without editing -- true, harmless, and unstated.
CONFIGURE_ARGS="--disable-static --disable-readline"
CFLAGS_EXTRA="-DSQLITE_ENABLE_FTS5=1 -DSQLITE_ENABLE_JSON1=1 \
-DSQLITE_ENABLE_COLUMN_METADATA=1 -DSQLITE_SECURE_DELETE=1"
